---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="What this reference is, who it's for, and how to contribute corrections."
  alternates={{ en: '/about', it: '/it/about' }}
>
  <article class="prose">
    <h1>About</h1>
    <p>
      <strong>Trasferirsi in Italia</strong> is a free, source-cited reference for
      moving to Italy. It exists because the information is real but scattered:
      official pages in Italian, out-of-date forum threads, and generic guides that
      gloss over the parts that actually trip people up.
    </p>

    <h2>Who it's for</h2>
    <p>
      Version 1 covers <strong>EU/EEA citizens</strong> establishing residency: the
      codice fiscale, registering at the comune (anagrafe), and enrolling in the
      national health service (SSN). We cover this slice deeply and keep it current,
      rather than covering everything shallowly.
    </p>

    <h2>How it's built to be trusted</h2>
    <p>
      Every page cites primary official sources and shows when it was last verified.
      See <a href="/how-we-verify">how we verify</a> for the full method. We work at
      the national, regional, and comune level to match how Italy's rules actually
      apply.
    </p>

    <h2 id="contribute">Contribute a correction</h2>
    <p>
      Rules change, and local readers often know before we do. If a page is out of
      date or you can point us to a better official source, please tell us — every
      page has a "suggest an update" link, or you can open an issue on the project.
      Reader reports feed straight into our review queue.
    </p>

    <h2>Disclaimer</h2>
    <p>
      This site is informational and is not legal or tax advice. Always confirm
      specifics with the relevant Italian authority before acting.
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
